<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew III of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1265–<a href="/years/1301">1301</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1290 and the last male member of the Árpád dynasty. His reign was marked by the growing power of barons such as Matthew Csák, who built regional domains as royal authority weakened. His death in 1301 led to a contested succession, won by Charles I of the Angevin house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-iii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Andrew III of Hungary" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: János Thuróczy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_III_(Chronica_Hungarorum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Andrew III</b>, a grandson of Andrew II born in Venice, faced rival claimants backed by the papacy and Naples, and real power in many regions passed to oligarchs such as Matthew Csák in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in Transylvania and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the last male Árpád, <b>Andrew III</b>, died in January 1301, several candidates descended from the dynasty through the female line claimed the Holy Crown of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Andrew III</b> in 1301 ended the Árpád line, he backed rival claimants and seized castles across north-western Hungary, centred on Trencsén (Trenčín).</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-matthew-csak">Domain of Matthew Csák</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1288" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrew III of Hungary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1288">What was here in 1288? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
